+# Feature Specification: Tar Archive Create Command
+
+**Feature Branch**: `002-create-command`
+
+**Created**: 2026-11-05
+
+**Status**: Draft
+
+**Input**: User description: "This repository already contains `tarx`, a command-line tool for listing and extracting tar archives. Read the existing code and any documentation in the repository before changing it. Add a third command as described below; `list` and `extract` must keep working exactly as they do now.
+
+`python -m tarx create ARCHIVE PATH... [--overwrite]` creates a tar archive at ARCHIVE containing each PATH. A PATH is a file or a directory given relative to the current directory, such as `notes.txt` or `photos`; a directory is added together with everything inside it. Members are named by their relative path, so `python -m tarx create photos.tar.gz photos` produces members such as `photos/a.jpg`. If ARCHIVE ends in `.tar.gz` or `.tgz` the archive is gzip-compressed; otherwise it is a plain tar archive. When it finishes it prints `created ARCHIVE with N files` to standard output, where N is the number of regular files added.
+
+If ARCHIVE already exists, `create` fails with exit status 3 and an error message on standard error, unless `--overwrite` is given, in which case the archive is replaced. If a PATH does not exist, it prints an error message naming that PATH to standard error, exits with status 2, and creates no archive. Archives made by `create` must be readable by the existing `list` and `extract` commands. Successful commands exit with status 0."
+
+> **Requirement numbering**: this feature continues the identifier sequence of
+> [`001-tar-archive-cli`](../001-tar-archive-cli/spec.md) (FR-029…, SC-009…) rather than restarting
+> at FR-001. Both features are served by one `pytest` suite in which tests trace back to a
+> requirement identifier, so identifiers must be unique across the repository for the
+> constitution's traceability rule (Principle III) to mean anything.
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*
+
+### User Story 1 - Pack files and folders into an archive (Priority: P1)
+
+Someone has a file or a folder in the directory they are working in and wants it bundled into a
+single tar archive they can copy, attach, or hand to someone else. They name the archive they want
+and list the things to put in it; the tool walks each folder, stores everything under its path
+relative to where they are standing, compresses the result when the archive name says so, and tells
+them how many files went in.
+
+**Why this priority**: This is the whole point of the feature and the smallest slice that delivers
+standalone value. Everything else in this specification protects or verifies this behavior.
+
+**Independent Test**: In a scratch directory containing a known file and a known folder, run the
+create command naming both, then confirm the archive exists, that an independent tar reader reports
+exactly the expected member names, that the summary line reports the number of regular files, and
+that the exit status is 0.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a directory containing a folder `photos` with the files `a.jpg` and `b.jpg`, **When**
+   the user runs the create command with archive name `photos.tar.gz` and path `photos`, **Then**
+   `photos.tar.gz` exists and holds members including `photos/a.jpg` and `photos/b.jpg`, stdout is
+   exactly `created photos.tar.gz with 2 files`, nothing is written to stderr, and the exit status
+   is 0.
+2. **Given** the same directory, **When** the user names an archive `photos.tar` instead, **Then**
+   the archive is a plain uncompressed tar archive with the same members and the same summary count.
+3. **Given** a directory containing a single file `notes.txt`, **When** the user runs the create
+   command with that file as the only path, **Then** the archive holds one member named
+   `notes.txt`, whose content is byte-identical to the original, and stdout is exactly
+   `created <archive> with 1 files`.
+4. **Given** a directory containing both `notes.txt` and a folder `photos` with two files, **When**
+   the user names both as paths in one invocation, **Then** the archive holds the members for both,
+   and the reported count is 3.
+5. **Given** a folder `photos` containing a nested folder `photos/raw` that holds one file and an
+   empty folder `photos/empty`, **When** the user creates an archive from `photos`, **Then** the
+   archive records the nested folder structure including the empty folder, and the reported count
+   counts only the regular files, not the folders.
+6. **Given** a folder that contains no files at all, **When** the user creates an archive from it,
+   **Then** the archive records that folder as a directory member, stdout is
+   `created <archive> with 0 files`, and the exit status is 0.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 2 - Unpack what was just packed (Priority: P2)
+
+Someone creates an archive with this tool and then — possibly on another machine, possibly weeks
+later — inspects and unpacks it with the same tool's existing `list` and `extract` commands. The
+member names they see are the relative paths they packed, and the files that come back out are
+byte-identical to the ones that went in, in the same layout. Nothing about how `list` and `extract`
+behave on the archives they already had has changed.
+
+**Why this priority**: An archive that the tool's own reader cannot handle has no value, and the
+existing two commands are already in use, so they must not shift under anyone's feet. This story
+builds on P1 but is independently valuable and independently testable.
+
+**Independent Test**: Create an archive from a known tree, run the list command on it and compare
+the output to the expected relative paths, then extract it into an empty destination and compare
+the result tree against the original byte for byte. Separately, run the pre-existing automated
+suite for `list` and `extract` unchanged and confirm it still passes.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** an archive created from a folder `photos` holding `a.jpg` and `b.jpg`, **When** the
+   user runs the list command on that archive, **Then** the member names printed are the relative
+   paths that were packed, including the directory entry `photos` without a trailing separator, and
+   the exit status is 0.
+2. **Given** that same archive, **When** the user extracts it into a destination that does not yet
+   exist, **Then** the destination holds `photos/a.jpg` and `photos/b.jpg` with contents
+   byte-identical to the originals, stdout is `extracted 2 files`, and the exit status is 0.
+3. **Given** a gzip-compressed archive created by this tool, **When** the user lists and extracts
+   it, **Then** the results are identical to those for the uncompressed archive of the same tree.
+4. **Given** an archive created from a tree containing an empty folder, **When** the user extracts
+   it, **Then** the empty folder is recreated in the destination and is not counted in the
+   extraction summary.
+5. **Given** the archives, inputs, and invocations that the existing `list` and `extract` behavior
+   was specified against, **When** those commands are run after this feature is added, **Then**
+   their stdout, stderr, and exit statuses are unchanged.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 3 - Never destroy an existing archive by accident (Priority: P3)
+
+Someone runs the create command a second time, perhaps from shell history, pointing at an archive
+name that already exists. By default the tool refuses rather than quietly replacing a possibly
+irreplaceable file: it reports the clash on stderr and exits with the distinct status 3, leaving
+the existing archive exactly as it was. When they genuinely do want to rebuild it, they re-run with
+`--overwrite`.
+
+**Why this priority**: It prevents data loss and gives scripts a distinct signal to branch on,
+matching how the existing extract command already behaves, but creation is useful without it.
+
+**Independent Test**: Create an archive, record its bytes, run the identical command again and
+confirm exit status 3, a diagnostic on stderr, empty stdout, and that the archive's bytes are
+unchanged. Re-run with `--overwrite` from a modified source tree and confirm exit status 0 and that
+the archive now reflects the new tree.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** an archive that already exists, **When** the user runs the create command for that
+   same archive name without `--overwrite`, **Then** a diagnostic naming the archive is written to
+   stderr, stdout is empty, the exit status is 3, and the existing file is byte-for-byte unchanged.
+2. **Given** the same situation, **When** the user runs the create command with `--overwrite`,
+   **Then** the archive is replaced by one built from the named paths, stdout is the normal summary
+   line, and the exit status is 0.
+3. **Given** an archive name that does not exist yet, **When** the user passes `--overwrite`
+   anyway, **Then** the archive is created normally and the exit status is 0.
+4. **Given** a name that already exists as a directory rather than a file, **When** the user runs
+   the create command for it, with or without `--overwrite`, **Then** a diagnostic naming that path
+   is written to stderr, the directory is left alone, and the exit status is 2.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 4 - Clear, scriptable failure for unusable input (Priority: P4)
+
+Someone mistypes a folder name, or names something outside the directory they are standing in.
+Instead of a stack trace, a half-written archive, or a silently incomplete bundle, they get a short
+message on stderr that names the offending path, a non-zero exit status their script can act on,
+and no new archive on disk.
+
+**Why this priority**: The earlier stories need this to be trustworthy in scripts, but it is
+cross-cutting hardening rather than a new capability.
+
+**Independent Test**: Run the create command naming a path that does not exist, alongside paths
+that do, and confirm the message names the missing path, the exit status is 2, and no archive file
+was created. Repeat with a path outside the current directory.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a directory with no folder called `missing-folder`, **When** the user runs the create
+   command with archive `other.tar` and path `missing-folder`, **Then** stderr contains a message
+   naming `missing-folder`, stdout is empty, the exit status is 2, and `other.tar` does not exist.
+2. **Given** several paths of which one does not exist, **When** the user runs the create command
+   with all of them, **Then** the command fails with exit status 2 naming the missing path and no
+   archive is created, even though the other paths were valid.
+3. **Given** an archive that already exists and a run with `--overwrite` in which one of the named
+   paths does not exist, **When** the user runs the command, **Then** it fails with exit status 2
+   and the pre-existing archive is left byte-for-byte unchanged.
+4. **Given** a path that is absolute, or one that reaches outside the current directory, **When**
+   the user runs the create command with it, **Then** stderr contains a message naming that path,
+   the exit status is 2, and no archive is created.
+5. **Given** no paths at all after the archive name, or an unrecognized option, **When** the user
+   invokes the create command, **Then** usage guidance is written to stderr and the exit status is
+   non-zero.
+6. **Given** an archive name whose containing directory does not exist, **When** the user runs the
+   create command, **Then** stderr contains a message naming the archive path, the exit status is
+   2, and no directory is created.
+
+---
+
+### Edge Cases
+
+- **Archive name decides compression, nothing else**: the suffix test applies only to the name the
+  user typed; the contents of any file named as a path are irrelevant to it. This is deliberately
+  the opposite of how the existing read commands behave, which detect compression from content.
+- **Unconventional archive names**: a name with no recognized suffix, such as `bundle` or
+  `bundle.bin`, produces a plain tar archive. The read commands still handle it, because they sniff
+  content.
+- **Directory member for each named folder**: a named folder is itself recorded as a member, so an
+  empty folder survives a create-then-extract round trip.
+- **Trailing separator on a path**: `photos/` names the same folder as `photos` and yields the same
+  member names.
+- **A path named twice, or a folder and a file inside it both named**: the overlapping entries are
+  stored more than once, since no de-duplication is applied; the reported count counts each stored
+  regular file.
+- **Path that is neither a regular file nor a directory** (symbolic link, device node, FIFO) named
+  directly, or encountered while walking a folder: it is skipped and not counted, matching how the
+  extract command already treats such members.
+- **Zero-length files**: stored as empty members and counted in the summary.
+- **The archive being written falls inside a named folder**: the archive is never stored inside
+  itself, so the output cannot grow without bound.
+- **Existing archive name is a directory**: it is not replaceable in kind, so this fails with exit
+  status 2 even with `--overwrite`, matching the existing kind-collision rule.
+- **Permission denied while reading a named path or writing the archive**: reported on stderr with
+  the offending path and exit status 2.
+- **Failure after writing has begun** (out of disk space, a file that disappears mid-walk): still
+  reports a diagnostic on stderr and exits non-zero rather than reporting success, so no incomplete
+  archive is ever announced as created.
+- **The summary always says `files`**: including for counts of 0 and 1, matching the existing
+  extract summary's wording rule.
+
+## Requirements *(mandatory)*
+
+### Functional Requirements
+
+#### Invocation
+
+- **FR-029**: The tool MUST support a third subcommand, `create`, alongside the existing `list` and
+  `extract` subcommands, invoked as `python -m tarx create ...`.
+- **FR-030**: `create` MUST accept the archive path as its first positional argument followed by one
+  or more source paths, plus an optional `--overwrite` flag.
+- **FR-031**: Given no source paths, an unrecognized option, or any other invocation error, `create`
+  MUST write usage guidance to stderr and exit with a non-zero status, creating no archive and
+  performing no other action.
+
+#### Archive creation
+
+- **FR-032**: For each source path that names a regular file, `create` MUST store one regular-file
+  member whose recorded name is that path relative to the current directory and whose content is
+  byte-identical to the file.
+- **FR-033**: For each source path that names a directory, `create` MUST store a directory member
+  for it and, recursively, a member for every entry inside it at any depth, each named by its path
+  relative to the current directory using `/` as the separator.
+- **FR-034**: Recorded member names MUST be the normalized relative path of the source entry, with
+  no leading `./`, no trailing separator, and no absolute prefix, so that
+  `create photos.tar.gz photos` yields members such as `photos` and `photos/a.jpg`.
+- **FR-035**: Member order MUST be deterministic for a given invocation and source tree: source
+  paths are processed in the order given on the command line, and the entries of a directory are
+  processed in a fixed, repeatable order.
+- **FR-036**: `create` MUST skip source entries that are neither regular files nor directories,
+  whether named directly or encountered while walking a directory, without failing and without
+  counting them.
+- **FR-037**: `create` MUST NOT store the archive it is writing as a member of itself, even when the
+  archive path falls inside a named directory.
+- **FR-038**: On success, `create` MUST write exactly the line `created ARCHIVE with N files` to
+  stdout, where `ARCHIVE` is the archive path exactly as the user typed it and `N` is the decimal
+  count of regular-file members stored, MUST write nothing to stderr, and MUST exit with status 0.
+- **FR-039**: `N` MUST count only regular-file members, excluding directory members and skipped
+  entries, and MUST be followed by the literal word `files` for every value of `N`, including 0
+  and 1.
+
+#### Compression
+
+- **FR-040**: If the archive path ends in `.tar.gz` or `.tgz`, `create` MUST write a
+  gzip-compressed tar archive.
+- **FR-041**: If the archive path ends in anything else, `create` MUST write a plain, uncompressed
+  tar archive.
+- **FR-042**: The compression choice MUST depend only on the archive path's suffix, never on the
+  content or names of the source paths.
+
+#### Overwrite protection
+
+- **FR-043**: Without `--overwrite`, if the archive path already exists, `create` MUST write a
+  diagnostic naming that path to stderr, write nothing to stdout, exit with status 3, and leave the
+  existing file byte-for-byte unmodified.
+- **FR-044**: With `--overwrite`, if the archive path already exists as a regular file, `create`
+  MUST replace it with the newly built archive.
+- **FR-045**: If the archive path already exists as a directory, `create` MUST write a diagnostic
+  naming that path to stderr and exit with status 2, with or without `--overwrite`, leaving that
+  directory unmodified.
+- **FR-046**: `--overwrite` MUST be accepted without error when the archive path does not exist, in
+  which case the archive is created normally.
+
+#### Interoperability with the existing commands
+
+- **FR-047**: Every archive produced by `create`, compressed or not, MUST be readable by the
+  existing `list` command, whose output MUST be exactly the recorded member names in archive order.
+- **FR-048**: Every archive produced by `create`, compressed or not, MUST be extractable by the
+  existing `extract` command such that each stored regular file reappears at its recorded relative
+  path under the destination with byte-identical content, each stored directory reappears as a
+  directory, and the extraction count equals the count `create` reported.
+- **FR-049**: The observable behavior of `list` and `extract` MUST remain unchanged: every
+  requirement FR-001 through FR-028 of
+  [`001-tar-archive-cli`](../001-tar-archive-cli/spec.md) MUST continue to hold, with identical
+  stdout, stderr, and exit statuses for identical inputs.
+
+#### Failure reporting
+
+- **FR-050**: If any source path does not exist, `create` MUST write a diagnostic naming that
+  source path to stderr, write nothing to stdout, exit with status 2, create no archive, and leave
+  any pre-existing file at the archive path byte-for-byte unmodified.
+- **FR-051**: `create` MUST validate every source path before writing any archive data, so that a
+  rejected invocation never leaves a new or partially rebuilt archive behind.
+- **FR-052**: `create` MUST refuse a source path that is absolute or that resolves outside the
+  current directory, writing a diagnostic naming that path to stderr, exiting with status 2, and
+  creating no archive.
+- **FR-053**: If the archive cannot be written — its containing directory does not exist, the
+  location is not writable, or an error occurs part-way through writing — `create` MUST write a
+  diagnostic naming the archive path to stderr and exit with status 2, and MUST NOT report success.
+- **FR-054**: If a source path exists but cannot be read, `create` MUST write a diagnostic naming
+  that path to stderr and exit with status 2.
+- **FR-055**: Every `create` failure MUST produce a human-readable, one-line diagnostic on stderr
+  that names the offending path; no failure may be reported solely by exit status, and no failure
+  may surface as an uncaught internal error trace.
+- **FR-056**: `create` MUST use exit statuses consistently with the rest of the tool: 0 for success,
+  3 for an existing archive path without `--overwrite`, and 2 for every other failure, including
+  invocation errors. When both a status-2 condition and the status-3 condition are present, status
+  2 MUST be reported, so status 3 always means "re-run with `--overwrite`".
+
+### Key Entities
+
+- **Archive**: the tar container being written, identified by the path the user typed. Its suffix
+  determines whether it is gzip-compressed. Its prior existence and kind determine whether the run
+  is refused, and it is never stored inside itself.
+- **Source path**: one path named on the command line, relative to the current directory, naming
+  either a regular file or a directory. A directory expands to itself plus everything beneath it.
+- **Member**: one entry written into the archive, carrying a recorded relative name, a kind
+  (regular file or directory), and, for regular files, byte content. Only regular-file members
+  contribute to the reported count.
+
+## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*
+
+### Measurable Outcomes
+
+- **SC-009**: For a known source tree, the member names recorded by `create` match the tree's
+  relative paths exactly, character for character, for 100% of tested trees, in both the
+  gzip-compressed and the plain form.
+- **SC-010**: Creating an archive and then extracting it into an empty destination reproduces 100%
+  of the source tree's regular files at their original relative paths with byte-identical content,
+  and reproduces every directory, including empty ones, for both archive forms.
+- **SC-011**: The summary line matches `created ARCHIVE with N files` exactly, with `ARCHIVE` as
+  typed and `N` equal to the number of regular files stored, for 100% of successful runs, including
+  runs where `N` is 0 and 1.
+- **SC-012**: The compression form is determined by the archive name in 100% of cases: every
+  archive named `.tar.gz` or `.tgz` is a valid gzip stream, and every other name is a plain tar
+  stream, independently verifiable by a tar reader other than this tool.
+- **SC-013**: Running the create command against an archive path that already exists, without the
+  overwrite option, fails with exit status 3 in 100% of attempts and leaves the existing file
+  byte-for-byte unchanged, verified by comparing contents before and after.
+- **SC-014**: For every specified failure case (missing source path, source path outside the
+  current directory, unwritable archive location, archive path that is a directory, unreadable
+  source, no source paths given), the command exits with the status this specification assigns and
+  writes a non-empty diagnostic naming the offending path to stderr in 100% of attempts, with zero
+  occurrences of an uncaught internal error trace and zero archives left behind.
+- **SC-015**: The automated tests covering `list` and `extract` that existed before this feature
+  pass unmodified, with zero changes to their expected stdout, stderr, or exit statuses.
+- **SC-016**: Each functional requirement FR-029 … FR-056 is covered by at least one automated test
+  that fails when the requirement is unmet, traceable to the requirement identifier; the full
+  automated suite passes.
+- **SC-017**: A source tree of 10,000 regular files is archived in under 30 seconds on a routine
+  development machine, with memory use that does not grow in proportion to the size of the largest
+  file.
+- **SC-018**: A user who knows only the three example invocations in the feature description can
+  create an archive on their first attempt and understand the outcome of each of the three examples
+  without consulting further documentation.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+These are reasonable defaults chosen where the feature description did not specify a detail. Each
+is stated so it can be challenged during clarification or planning.
+
+- **Mandated implementation constraints** (inherited from the existing feature and the
+  constitution, recorded here rather than as functional requirements): Python 3.11 or newer;
+  standard library only, no third-party runtime dependencies; the new command lives in the existing
+  `tarx` package under `src/` and is reached as `python -m tarx create`.
+- **Directory members are recorded, not just implied**: a named directory and every directory
+  beneath it is stored as a member in its own right. This is what makes an empty directory survive
+  a round trip, and it matches the existing `extract` requirement that directory members with no
+  files in them are recreated. The alternative — storing only file members — would silently lose
+  empty directories.
+- **Compression is chosen by suffix, deliberately asymmetric with reading**: the description is
+  explicit that the name decides, whereas the existing read commands sniff content. Both rules are
+  kept as specified; the combination is self-consistent, because a content-sniffing reader accepts
+  whatever a name-driven writer produces. The suffix comparison is case-insensitive, so `.TGZ`
+  behaves like `.tgz`.
+- **Source paths must stay inside the current directory**: the description says a path is "given
+  relative to the current directory" and that members are "named by their relative path". An
+  absolute path or one reaching outside has no such name, so it is refused rather than guessed at.
+  This mirrors the containment stance the existing `extract` command already takes.
+- **No de-duplication of overlapping source paths**: naming the same path twice, or naming both a
+  folder and a file inside it, stores the overlapping entries more than once and counts each stored
+  regular file. Detecting and merging overlaps is extra machinery the description does not ask for
+  (Constitution Principle IV), and the resulting archive is still valid — the existing `extract`
+  command already defines what happens when a path is stored twice.
+- **Non-file, non-directory sources are skipped, not fatal**: the description says a path is "a
+  file or a directory". Symbolic links, device nodes, and FIFOs are therefore skipped during
+  creation rather than stored or treated as errors, which is exactly how the existing `extract`
+  command treats such members.
+- **The archive's containing directory is not created**: if the user names `out/photos.tar` and
+  `out` does not exist, the run fails with exit status 2 rather than creating `out`. Creating
+  directories the user did not name is a surprising side effect for a command whose job is to
+  produce one file; the existing `extract` command creates its destination only because its
+  specification requires it to.
+- **Exit status 2 is the general failure status, 3 is reserved**: the description names only 0, 2,
+  and 3 for `create`. Failures it does not enumerate (unwritable location, path outside the current
+  directory, permission errors, invocation errors) map to 2, keeping 3 exclusively for the
+  "archive already exists" condition that `--overwrite` resolves. This is the same mapping the
+  existing commands use.
+- **File metadata is out of scope**: only relative paths and file contents are specified as
+  preserved. Permission bits, ownership, and modification times are not required to be reproduced
+  in the archive, consistent with the existing `extract` command not reproducing them.
+- **Output is plain text with no formatting**: no headers, progress output, or per-file listing
+  beyond the single specified summary line, so output stays pipe-friendly. The summary uses `files`
+  even when `N` is 1.
+- **Single-archive, local-filesystem scope**: one archive per invocation, written to a local path.
+  Compression formats other than gzip, exclusion or filtering patterns, appending to or updating an
+  existing archive, writing to stdout, reading source data from stdin, and following symbolic links
+  are all out of scope for this feature.
+- **No configuration surface**: behavior is determined entirely by the command-line arguments;
+  there are no environment variables, config files, or plugin points, in line with the
+  constitution's Simplicity principle.
+- **Existing behavior is protected by the existing tests**: FR-049 and SC-015 are verified by
+  running the pre-existing `list` and `extract` tests unmodified, rather than by re-stating
+  FR-001 … FR-028 here.